                <pb facs="0613" n="515" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg515"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1121 Smit, Albert H./14: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-03-09T12:00:00+08:00">March 9,
                        1930—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received March 9—6:32 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>184. Following from Minister Johnson dated Shanghai, March 8, 2 p.m.: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Please repeat the following to the Department: ‘Your 84, March 4, 7
                                p.m.<anchor n="10" xml:id="d571-f1"/> I share the feeling of the
                            District Attorney that the investigation should proceed, and recommend
                            that requisition of funds be arranged as outlined in Porter’s telegram
                            of March 3, 4 p.m.,<anchor n="10" xml:id="d571-f2"/> it being desirable
                            (1) that all evidence should be before the district attorney for the
                            purpose of determining whether there is any basis for criminal
                            prosecution and (2) in order to show that a thorough investigation has
                            been made’”.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
